\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For $G=Spin(2n)$ over C, $n\geq 2$, and dominant integral weights $\lambda ,\mu ,\nu $ whose sum belongs to the root lattice, does the existence of a G-invariant in $V(N\lambda )\otimes V(N\mu )\otimes V(N\nu )$ for some integer $N\geq 1$ imply the existence of one in $V(\lambda )\otimes V(\mu )\otimes V(\nu )$?

\subsection{Short English statement}
For $G=Spin(2n)$ over C, $n\geq 2$, and dominant integral weights $\lambda ,\mu ,\nu $ whose sum belongs to the root lattice, does the existence of a G-invariant in $V(N\lambda )\otimes V(N\mu )\otimes V(N\nu )$ for some integer $N\geq 1$ imply the existence of one in $V(\lambda )\otimes V(\mu )\otimes V(\nu )$?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] OpenAI, manuscript citation page. Source of the formulation motivating this local problem.
\url{https://github.com/openai/math/blob/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/preprints/Tensor-Saturation-for-Even-Spin-Groups-September-24-2026/README.md}.
\end{itemize}

